, "deploy/curation.service", "deploy/nas/compose.example.yaml", "docs/source-plugins.md", "tests/preferences/test_citations_resolve.py", "tests/preferences/test_deployment_values.py", "tests/preferences/test_seam_foreign_keys.py", "tests/preferences/test_seam_imports.py", "tests/test_actions_pinned.py", "tests/test_default_suite_ci_scope.py", "tests/test_repo_hygiene.py"], "files_oracle": [".prawduct/artifacts/accessibility-spec.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/architecture.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/archive/build-plan.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/boundary-patterns.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-audit-wave-1.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/clients.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/data-model.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/dezoomify-cli-findings.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/information-architecture.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/nonfunctional-requirements.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/observability-strategy.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/operational-spec.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/player-contract.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/project-preferences.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/re-architecture.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/security-model.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/source-plugins.md", ".prawduct/changes/audit-wave-1.md", ".prawduct/operator-verification.md"], "findings": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "No test evidence for the reviewed head: the recorded run is of the bundle's base tree", "recommendation": "`prawduct-hook test-status` reports stale: the evidence (2026-11-18T22:25:54Z, 8021 passed) predates this session, and its `evidence_tree` is c7ab594b, which is the manifest's `base_tree`. So no recorded run covers any of the 99 commits under review, including the FX-A/FX-B integration fixes and the test edits merged after them. Chunk 11's Done-when needs the curation, browser, governance and contract suites, plus the UX walk, to pass on the merged head. Run `testament run --json` on f676e395 and record it (`prawduct-hook test-evidence record`, or `--from-junit`) before merging. This is an instance: one stale record.", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-audit-wave-1.md"], "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "warning", "title": "seed and identify write the pre-migration copy beside the catalogue even when BACKUP_DIR is set", "recommendation": "Only `__main__.py` passes `snapshot_directory=settings.backup_dir`. `arrt/src/arrt/seed/__main__.py:47` and `arrt/src/arrt/identify/__main__.py:39` call `open_catalogue_file(settings.catalogue_path, wall_name=...)` with no directory, so `file.py` falls back to the catalogue's own directory. Meanwhile `operational-spec.md` \u00a7 Backup and Restore, `deploy/README.md` (\"Before an upgrade that drops something\") and `.env.example` all say the copy goes to BACKUP_DIR when it is set. `.env.example` also says to keep backups on storage other than ART_ROOT's. Here is what goes wrong: an operator runs `identify` or `seed` as the first open after an upgrade. The copy then lands on the catalogue's own disk. The server's later start finds nothing left to drop and takes no copy. The rollback the spec describes then sends the operator to a BACKUP_DIR that holds no pre-migration copy. Scope: class, the callers of `open_catalogue_file` that hold a `Settings` (three today, one wired). Pass `settings.backup_dir` at both sites, or have `open_catalogue_file` take the setting so a caller cannot forget it. Pin it with a test that runs a caller with BACKUP_DIR set ('When a behaviour matters because something calls or reads it, test through that caller with a non-default value').", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/seed/__main__.py", "arrt/src/arrt/identify/__main__.py", "arrt/src/arrt/persistence/file.py"], "fid": "R-2"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "A crash loop whose first drop fails replaces the copy from before the upgrade with a copy of the already-migrated file", "recommendation": "`_drop_if_alike` deletes the newest earlier copy whenever the new copy would drop the same things. The store's open commits a lot before the first drop runs: the widening, the schema, `establish_the_wall`, `mark_the_default_theme` and `rename_awaiting_to_wanted` (a value rewrite). Suppose the first dropping step fails on every start; `_require_drop_column` on an SQLite without DROP COLUMN is the documented case. Then start 2 finds the same drops, copies the already widened and rewritten file, and deletes start 1's copy, which was the only file 'as the previous release left it' (`operational-spec.md`). That is exactly the crash loop the mid-build DECISION says this rule protects. The rollback the spec prescribes (restore the copy, run the previous release) then hands the previous release a file it never wrote. The one test, `test_a_migration_failing_on_every_start_never_pushes_out_the_copy_from_before_it`, covers only the case where the first start's drops succeed, so the drops differ and both copies are kept ('When an artifact works a rule on two cases, derive the rule from one and check the other'). Scope: instance. When the drops match, keep the first copy of a run of matching copies and replace only the later ones. Add the identical-drops case, failing at `retire_wall_tokens`, asserting that the surviving copy still has the pre-widening shape.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/persistence/backup.py", "arrt/tests/unit/test_pre_migration_snapshot.py"], "fid": "R-3"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "Two hand-rolled act scopes keyed on thread ident, one of which detects an open transaction through after_commit's side effect", "recommendation": "Class. This branch introduced the same pattern twice, with no shared owner: CatalogueService._announcing_together (_gathered and _gathering_thread) and DisplayService._publishing (_staged and _staging_thread). Each puts per-act mutable state on a shared service, keyed on threading.get_ident() (8 sites at head, 0 at base). The two copies already disagree. _publishing refuses to open inside a transaction it did not open, while _announcing_together joins one. In display.py the two-attribute predicate is spelled again at five sites, in three different forms (lines 487, 509, 2292, 2776, and inside _publishing). _publishing also learns whether a store transaction is open by registering a no-op after_commit callback and checking whether it ran (_outside_a_transaction). To make that possible, ProgrammingStore's protocol was widened with after_commit, which Programming uses only as this probe. The consequence: wave 2's store split (#216) gives Programming a store of its own. If that store's after_commit always runs callbacks at once, the guard silently passes inside a foreign transaction and feeds are written before that transaction decides anything. A fix made to one act scope (for example moving to contextvars) also does not reach the other. Remedy by construction: give the store one primitive, either an in_transaction() query or a transaction() that yields an act object with a slot for act-local state. Both services use it, and display.py reads its act through one helper. To bound the class, search arrt/src for get_ident() and for after_commit used as a probe.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/programming/display.py", "arrt/src/arrt/library/services/catalogue.py", "arrt/src/arrt/programming/store.py"], "fid": "R-4"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "DisplayService.sync still publishes any theme without recording a hang; the audit-5 F1 hazard is closed only at the MCP registry", "recommendation": "Instance. DisplayService.sync(wall_id, theme_id=None) (display.py:1958) still builds and publishes whatever theme it is given, with no assignment recorded. The branch closed the defect by having the MCP registry refuse theme_id, so the dangerous capability is still public on the service. Tests still use it as a shortcut: test_player_contract.py:51 and :135, and test_manifest.py:319 and :332, call display.sync(wall_id, theme.id). That is the same shortcut the plan's DECISION retired from the MCP tests. The next surface to call the service (an HTTP route, an Ask tool) reopens the defect, and no test at the service level would notice. The binding stays thin either way: make the public sync take wall_id alone, and move the theme-taking path that activate_theme and the hang-a-selection act use (display.py:1101 and :1150) to a private method. Change those four test setups to hang first, as the MCP tests now do. Their assertions stay as they are.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/programming/display.py"], "fid": "R-5"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "\"Shown by a Player\" is exported as one rule and then re-derived beside it; the list joiner now has four copies", "recommendation": "Class, in arrt/src/arrt/http/static. The branch exports shownByAPlayer from core/hanging.js as the shared rule for whether a Player shows a wall (audit finding 19), and core/selection.js uses it. The new notReported in screens/walls.js (line 1133) works the same question out again with its own expression: !wall.client_id || display_state.state === 'unassigned'. The pre-existing stateBadge (line 523) works it out a third way. The plan names 'the one health model across top bar, Walls, Status and Players' as wave 2. That work has to find every one of these derivations, and until it does, the Hang confirmation and the Walls card can disagree about the same wall. The 'a, b and c' joiner has the same problem. The branch added listedNames in core/hanging.js, about 70 lines above an identical inline listed lambda in the same file (line 106), and added listedWords in screens/walls.js. clients.js already had its own listed. Remedy: notReported, and stateBadge where it means the same thing, call shownByAPlayer; one exported joiner replaces the four. To bound the class, search static/ for '\"unassigned\"' and for slice(0, -1).join(\", \").", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/http/static/screens/walls.js", "arrt/src/arrt/http/static/core/hanging.js"], "fid": "R-6"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "blocking", "title": "record-lint chunk-ref-missing: chunk 09:823", "recommendation": "declared deliverable `deploy/curation.service` file does not exist", "source": "record-lint", "fid": "R-7"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "blocking", "title": "record-lint chunk-ref-missing: chunk 09:829", "recommendation": "declared deliverable `deploy/curation.service` file does not exist", "source": "record-lint", "fid": "R-8"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "blocking", "title": "record-lint chunk-ref-missing: chunk 09:857", "recommendation": "declared deliverable `deploy/curation.service` file does not exist", "source": "record-lint", "fid": "R-9"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "warning", "title": "record-lint change-fragment: .prawduct/changes/audit-wave-1.md", "recommendation": "(1) h2-in-body: '## What an operator must know': only `## Consumer note` is a `##` heading here; use `###`, because `##` starts a new entry once the fragment is archived; (2) h2-in-body: '## What else changed for someone running the server': only `## Consumer note` is a `##` heading here; use `###`, because `##` starts a new entry once the fragment is archived; (3) h2-in-body: '## Not done, and why': only `## Consumer note` is a `##` heading here; use `###`, because `##` starts a new entry once the fragment is archived", "files": [".prawduct/changes/audit-wave-1.md"], "source": "record-lint", "fid": "R-10"}], "counts": {"blocking": 3, "warning": 7, "note": 0}, "observations": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Unintended", "title": "Two built departures are missing from the plan's 'Decisions made mid-build'", "recommendation": "Chunk 04's M7 deliverable says curator-facing text calls the Original the 'original image'. What was built is a new \u00a7 Vocabulary entry, 'picture' and 'copy for the walls', across the server's sentences and the MCP descriptions. Separately, FX-B added `pictured_artwork_id` to `http/api.py`, `http/models.py`, `registry_search.py` and the MCP search. The plan's 'Shared seams' section said nobody edits `api.py` or `models.py` in this wave. Both are recorded elsewhere: the IA open question asks the owner, and `api-contract.md` and the change fragment say 'additive'. So neither is undecided. But the plan says its mid-build section is 'the later word', and someone vetoing from it sees neither. Add one [DECISION] line each.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/http/models.py", "arrt/src/arrt/library/services/registry_search.py", "arrt/src/arrt/library/services/thumbnails.py"], "oid": "O-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "image.yml's IMAGE_INPUTS says 'one path or directory per line', but its match is exact per line", "recommendation": "`grep -Fxq -f` matches whole lines only, so a directory added to IMAGE_INPUTS would never match the files under it, and that pull request would skip the build without a word. Every entry today is a file, so nothing is wrong yet. Either say 'one file per line', or match a directory entry as a prefix. `tests/test_default_suite_ci_scope.py` already reads this list, so it could refuse a directory entry.", "files": [".github/workflows/image.yml"], "oid": "O-2"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Unintended", "title": "preparation.py's lock is justified by a fixed staging name that chunk 04 removed", "recommendation": "The `_one_at_a_time` docstring says the queue and a regenerate 'both stage the master at one fixed name'. `make_master` now writes through `atomic.write_bytes` under a name of its own per attempt. The lock still earns its place because of the double mat spend and the duplicate work, so cut the stale half of the reason rather than adding to it.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/library/acquisition/preparation.py"], "oid": "O-3"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Unintended", "title": "Governing-Artifact Reconciliation: every disposition checked holds", "recommendation": "Each claim below was checked against the code, and no departure was found. Architecture: `preview` is one `build_manifest` call. `sync` passes only the wall. `atomic.py` imports from neither side of the seam. `WorkChanged.work_ids` carries a bulk act as one event. No route was added. Security \u00a7 Direction: none of the static diff's added lines use innerHTML, outerHTML or insertAdjacentHTML. The inventory now has TESTAMENT_KEY and the CI OpenRouter key. Design direction: no raw colour was added to app.css. Data-model: a rejected scan is skipped at promotion, and rejecting one deselects it, so a primary is never skipped. Nonfunctional: spend is recorded before the mat write, and no meter was added. Observability: QuietPlayerPolls drops only Player-route 2xx/304 lines. Operational-spec: the copy and the start-up skip exist, though see the two findings above on where the copy lands and which copy survives. No change needed beyond those findings.", "oid": "O-4"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "Shipped comments cite audit findings whose reports are not in this public repository, and narrate history", "recommendation": "Class. 25 lines under arrt/src cite '(audit 6, finding 13)', '(audit-8 F10)', '(audit 2026-11-19, finding 2)', 'the 2026-11-19 UX walk's finding 2' and similar, against 0 at the base. Many browser tests carry the same citations. The reports sit in the gitignored .ux-walk/audit-2026-11-19/ of one checkout, so no reader of this public repo can resolve one. Several of these comments also narrate history, for example 'It wore the act's bordered shape until 2026-11-19' (router.js) and 'Until 2026-11-19 each feed was written as it was built' (display.py _publishing). Remedy: delete the citations and the history clauses and keep the stated why, which every one of them already gives inline. To bound the class, grep -iE \"audit[- ]?[0-9]|audit 2026|walk's finding\" over arrt/src and arrt/tests. Rated as an observation: the why is present in each comment, so no maintainer acts wrongly because of the dangling id.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/http/static/screens/walls.js", "arrt/src/arrt/http/static/core/router.js", "arrt/src/arrt/programming/display.py", "arrt/src/arrt/programming/clients.py", "arrt/src/arrt/library/acquisition/preparation.py"], "oid": "O-5"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "The pool's run deadline adds a second thread-per-call mechanism for a finder that production never hands it", "recommendation": "ImageSourcePool._every_answer replaces the executor with one daemon thread per source and a backstop deadline, 'for any finder handed to it directly'. The only production construction is container.py:291, ImageSourcePool(sources.finders), and every one of those finders is already a _ContainedFinder bounded by loading._Calls. The backstop therefore guards only test finders. It re-implements _Calls' wait, discard and log-late behaviour without its straggler cap, so an unwrapped finder that hangs on every work would add one thread per run. Either keep the executor and rely on the containment, or state in the pool's docstring that the backstop's scope is test-only. No edit is required if the defence in depth is wanted.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/library/discovery/pool.py"], "oid": "O-6"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "StorageFault is documented as a fault of the host, but wraps every OperationalError", "recommendation": "_host_faults turns every sqlite3.OperationalError into StorageFault. That includes 'no such column', 'no such table' and syntax errors from a raw query() statement, and these are code or schema faults, not the host's. StorageFault's docstring says 'The file could not be read or written at all: a fault of the host'. The queue pauses on such a fault every 15 minutes indefinitely, and its remedy tells the operator to close other programs or restart Arrt. The mid-build DECISION accepts 'every OperationalError' for the queue, so this is not raised as a finding. If it is revisited, sqlite_errorcode (SQLITE_BUSY, LOCKED, FULL, IOERR, READONLY, CANTOPEN) narrows the type to what its docstring claims.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/persistence/durable.py", "arrt/src/arrt/persistence/errors.py", "arrt/src/arrt/library/acquisition/queue.py"], "oid": "O-7"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "Rationale this branch made stale: the preparation lock's fixed staging name, atomic's '.tmp' claim, and the compose file's Pi unit", "recommendation": "Three comments describe things this branch changed. (1) The docstring of PreparationService._one_at_a_time says 'both stage the master at one fixed name, so one's replace can move the other's half-written file into place'. master.py now writes through atomic.write_bytes under a unique name, so that half of the reason is gone. The lock is still justified by the duplicate mat spend, and the docstring can say only that. (2) atomic.py's module docstring says a staged name 'ends .tmp'. A keep_suffix name ends .partial<suffix>, which sweep_stale also handles. (3) deploy/nas/compose.example.yaml's 'In place of the Pi unit's MemoryMax' refers to deploy/curation.service, which this branch deleted. All three are wording only, and no edit is wanted beyond deleting the stale clause.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/library/acquisition/preparation.py", "arrt/src/arrt/atomic.py", "deploy/nas/compose.example.yaml"], "oid": "O-8"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "The plugin guide's browser example declares less than the guide says such a plugin needs", "recommendation": "docs/source-plugins.md shows a browser plugin declaring deadlines={'finder': 600, 'reader': 600}. Its \u00a7 Deadlines then says 'a browser plugin whose page may take 1,012 seconds, queue included, declares at least that'. An author who copies the example gets slow pages answered 'could not be asked'. Either make the example's numbers relational ('your measured worst case') or match them to the stated bound.", "files": ["docs/source-plugins.md"], "oid": "O-9"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "take_pre_migration_snapshot re-implements migrations.open_existing inline", "recommendation": "backup.py:289-290 repeats open_existing's read-write connect and row_factory, and its comment points at open_existing for the reason. Calling open_existing keeps the hot-journal rule in one place. This is cheap and can go in the next commit that touches either file.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/persistence/backup.py"], "oid": "O-10"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "Two operator-verification entries still lean on the deleted unit and its deleted tests", "recommendation": "Records Pass, below every bar. The closed entry 'The loader unit starts clean with its declared EnvironmentFile=' (around line 2992) says the live units `display.service` and `curation.service` 'are held to the same EnvironmentFile= rule by tests/test_repo_hygiene.py' and that this is 'the durable form of this check'. This branch deleted both the unit and those five tests, and chunk 10's scan edited a line in that same entry. The Pending wave-2b Chunk 04 soak (step 4, around line 1662) still says `sudo systemctl stop curation.service`. Neither clears a bar: the first entry is closed with 'do not run it', and the second is marked as replaced by build-plan-clients.md Chunk 05, so nobody follows either one. This is an instance of the learnings rule 'When you retire, amend, descope or close anything, first grep the whole repo (no --include) for its nouns and OLD wording'. Instance fix if you want one: delete the guard sentence in the closed entry. No edit is owed.", "files": [".prawduct/operator-verification.md"], "oid": "O-11"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "The Frame-move WARNING has no event key, unlike every other journal line this wave added", "recommendation": "`ClientService._say_moved` logs the move at WARNING with a message only, no `extra={\"event\": ...}`. Every other line this wave added does carry an event (source.plugin_deadline, source.plugin_late, source.claims_overlap, image_pool.late, acquisition.queue_host_fault, backup.skipped_recent, catalogue.snapshot_*), and observability-strategy.md lists those but not this one. security-model.md and clients.md say an operator 'can find a move nobody made in the journal'. That holds for a text search, but a journal query by event cannot find it. The module carries no event extras anywhere, so this matches its local style. Naming one (for example `client.display_moved`) and listing it would make the promise queryable. Instance.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/programming/clients.py"], "oid": "O-12"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "Snapshot pruning after a successful copy has no handling of its own", "recommendation": "In `take_pre_migration_snapshot`, `_drop_if_alike` and `_retain_snapshots` call `unlink` after the copy has been written and checked, outside the try that turns a failure into SnapshotFailed with `catalogue.snapshot_failed`. An OSError there would leave the server without a start, with a bare traceback in place of the named remedy, even though the copy it needed exists. This is unlikely, because the same directory has just accepted a write, but the copy is already safe at that point. Suppressing and logging a failed prune, as `atomic.sweep_stale` does for its debris, would match the rest of the module. Instance.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/persistence/backup.py"], "oid": "O-13"}], "duration_seconds": 580, "scope": "audit-wave-1", "scope_chosen_by": "binding", "scope_unresolved_cause": null, "scope_reason": null, "base_extended_from": null, "chunks": ["01", "02", "03", "04", "06", "07", "08", "09", "10", "11"], "chunks_proposed": ["01", "02", "03", "04", "06", "07", "08", "09", "10", "11"], "base_reviewed": "c19bc678d5f6ce890818f0b93a2df6707adda9a4", "record_lint": {"records": [".prawduct/artifacts/accessibility-spec.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/architecture.md